<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernard-Roger of Foix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1036</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernard-Roger was a son of Count Roger I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> who, on the division of his father's lands around 1012, received the region around Foix together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a>. His son Roger took the title of count of Foix, founding the line of counts that later inherited Béarn and the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernard-roger-of-foix.jpg" alt="Bernard-Roger of Foix" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_province_fr_Couserans.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
